<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sacred Cenote</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cenote of Sacrifice</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sacred Cenote is a large natural sinkhole at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a> in Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, linked to the city's main plaza by a raised causeway. The Maya threw offerings into it, including gold, jade, copal, pottery and human victims, as gifts to the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaac</a>, and pilgrims still visited it at the Spanish conquest. Dredging and diving since the early 20th century, beginning with Edward Thompson, have recovered thousands of objects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote.jpg" alt="Sacred Cenote" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salhedine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cenote_Xtoloc_en_Chich%C3%A9n_Itz%C3%A1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a> is a limestone plain with no rivers, streams, lakes or ponds, pocked with natural sinkholes, called cenotes, that expose the water table. The Sacred Cenote, also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> of Sacrifice, is 60 metres in diameter, with sheer cliffs dropping some 27 metres to the water, and the sacbe that joins it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>'s civic precinct is 300 metres long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Friar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, wrote in 1566 that the people had the custom of throwing men alive into the well as a sacrifice in times of drought, believing that they did not die, and that if the country had possessed gold, this well would hold the greater part of it. His account does not show how regular the practice was.</p><p>Besides gold and jade, the finds include flint, obsidian, shell, wood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, cloth and human skeletons. Jade is the largest category, followed by textiles, and the water preserved wooden weapons, sceptres, idols, tools and jewellery. None of the jade, gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> is native to Yucatán, so these were valued goods brought from elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. Many objects were deliberately damaged before being thrown in, which archaeologists have speculated was a way of killing them as offerings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Most of the major finds were made under Edward Herbert Thompson, who, with money from Stephen Salisbury III, began dredging with a bucket on a pulley in 1904; much of his material is at the Peabody Museum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>. In 1909 he dived to the bottom with two Greek divers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and found gold objects, jades and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> bells in the mud of its cracks. Salisbury's death, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> and a fire that destroyed some of his notes interrupted the work, which ended by 1923.</p><p>In 1961 the Instituto Nacional de Antropologia e Historia, with the National Geographic Society and CEDAM, recovered an inscribed gold-sheathed bone, a chert knife with a gold-sheathed wooden handle and wooden ear flares with jade and turquoise mosaic. In 1967–1968 Norman Scott and Román Piña Chán tried to empty and clarify the water, with only partial success.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sacred Cenote” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Maya cities, where in 378 its warriors installed a new dynasty at Tikal; after Teotihuacan’s fall, the cult of the Feathered Serpent flourished at Tula, the city of the Toltecs, and at Chichén Itzá in Yucatán, whose <b>Sacred Cenote</b> received offerings of jade, gold and human lives.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Caves, as entrances to the underworld, and cenotes, as sources of water and rain, were sacred: the <b>Sacred Cenote</b> at Chichén Itzá received offerings of jade, gold, copal incense and human lives.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>A causeway 270 metres long leads to the <b>Sacred Cenote</b>, 60 metres across, its water some 27 metres below sheer cliffs.</blockquote><a href="/work/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Sacred Cenote</b> received offerings of gold, jade and human sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a></li></ul></section>
